1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,866
Outlook was down for about a day.

2
00:00:03,046 --> 00:00:05,191
And it wasn't a hack.

3
00:00:05,591 --> 00:00:06,594
On November 12,

4
00:00:06,594 --> 00:00:09,739
Microsoft's email servers started failing their

5
00:00:09,739 --> 00:00:12,281
own safety checks and dropped offline.

6
00:00:12,461 --> 00:00:16,352
The rest got overloaded and failed too.

7
00:00:16,532 --> 00:00:18,313
Outlook and Teams went

8
00:00:18,313 --> 00:00:20,093
down around the world.

9
00:00:20,273 --> 00:00:23,251
Microsoft hasn't published the full cause yet.

10
00:00:23,651 --> 00:00:24,509
Next time,

11
00:00:24,509 --> 00:00:27,596
check Microsoft's status page first.

12
00:00:27,776 --> 00:00:30,028
Expect late emails.

13
00:00:30,208 --> 00:00:31,860
Move anything urgent to

14
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:33,225
a call or WhatsApp.

15
00:00:33,625 --> 00:00:35,794
Should you switch to Google?

16
00:00:35,974 --> 00:00:36,621
You can,

17
00:00:36,621 --> 00:00:38,643
but Google goes down too,

18
00:00:38,643 --> 00:00:41,392
and moving a company is expensive.

19
00:00:41,572 --> 00:00:44,455
A backup plan costs far less.

20
00:00:44,855 --> 00:00:47,436
You can't stop Microsoft from breaking,

21
00:00:47,436 --> 00:00:50,018
but you can keep your business running.

22
00:00:50,198 --> 00:00:51,916
Pick a second channel,

23
00:00:51,916 --> 00:00:53,947
keep key contacts offline,

24
00:00:53,947 --> 00:00:55,587
and practise it once.

25
00:00:55,987 --> 00:00:57,502
What did your office do

26
00:00:57,502 --> 00:00:59,017
when Outlook went down?

27
00:00:59,017 --> 00:01:00,598
Tell me in the comments.
